migration:
One index: `pack_confirmations_created_idx (tenant_id, created_at, id)`, declared in schema/warehouse.ts. It is a single drizzle-generated, expand-only migration at the next free index after DOS-032+059's 0043/0044, with no hand-written sibling (no table, policy, grant, trigger or FORCE change; precedent 0041/0042).

Why it cannot be avoided:
- The fix moves EVERY packs.list call to `created_at desc, id desc`, and pack_confirmations has no index containing created_at. Its indexes are the id PK, (tenant_id, updated_at) and (tenant_id, order_id).
- So each call becomes a top-N sort of the tenant's whole pack history. That is one row per order, for ever.
- The callers:
  - W1 home on every open (warehouse-app/app/index.tsx:57-60, invoiced=false);
  - W5 (pack/index.tsx:33-42);
  - M20 (manager-app/app/fulfilment/pack.tsx:95);
  - the manager billing desk (billing/index.tsx:107).
- EXPLAIN ANALYZE on dos_qa: 848 tarsun packs, Seq Scan + top-N heapsort, 4.3 ms. That is linear in history, against docs/20 rule 3 ('no endpoint ever loads a whole tenant's history').
- DOS-023 could skip an index because picklists already had picklists_status_idx (tenant_id, status, created_at); pack_confirmations has nothing comparable.

The awaiting_load predicate itself needs nothing new:
- the packed set comes from `sales_orders_state_idx (tenant_id, state, created_at)` (schema/orders.ts:114);
- the join uses `pack_confirmations_order_uniq (tenant_id, order_id)` (schema/warehouse.ts:174);
- the live-sheet anti-join is create's existing cost class. EXPLAIN ANALYZE on dos_qa: Hash Join from sales_orders state=packed, nested-loop anti join over 83 live sheets, 3.5 ms.
